  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1200 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\nTuesday through Sunday:\n\nSynopsis: High pressure will continue to shift east of the region on \nTuesday, with southerly return flow allowing for gradual moisture \nreturns and slight increases in humidity. The next upper disturbance \nand surface front will drift into the western Great Lakes from the \nCanadian Prairies Tuesday evening through Wednesday, bringing the \nnext chances for showers and thunderstorms to southern Wisconsin. A \nnew area of high pressure will move into the western Great Lakes \nduring the second half of the week, leading to pleasant and mostly \ndry weather conditions Thursday into Friday. The high will shift \neast by Saturday, with returning southerly flow leading to steady \nincreases in humidity through the remainder of next weekend. \nConcurrent increases in zonal upper level flow, in addition to \nembedded shortwaves, will support the next shower and storm chances \nwithin the returning moisture. Saturday night into Sunday is the \ncurrently favored window for storms in southern Wisconsin, with more \nprecise timing clarifying through this week. \n\nTuesday Evening through Wednesday: Still anticipating periods of \nshowers and storms across southern Wisconsin as an upper wave &\nattendant surface cold front move through the western Great \nLakes. General idea of initial (likely elevated) storms within \nan increasing warm advection regime Tuesday evening/night, \nfollowed by a window of surface-based storm potential along the \npassing cold front Wednesday afternoon, remains on track in this\nforecast. Latest six hourly precip probs reflect these \nexpectations. Whether or not any of this activity reaches severe\nlevels remains less certain, however, largely due to the \ncurrently progged timing of the best synoptic-scale ascent. \nGlobal & longer-range mesoscale guidance is relatively insistent\non a Tuesday overnight/early day Wednesday passage of the upper\nwave & surface front, which would be misaligned with peak \nsurface heating & the best instability. Nevertheless, prevailing\ndeep layer shear will be supportive of more organized storm \ndevelopment through this time frame, with any combination of \nfactors promoting increased instability leading to higher \npotential for a few severe storms with hail and gusty winds. \nWill be monitoring setup and timing details closely over the \nnext 24-36 hours. \n\nSaturday Night through Sunday: A second window for showers and \nstorms opens as low level moisture deepens & the mid-upper pattern \nbecomes more energetic. More precise details will clarify through \nthis week, though it does appear that periods of storms will be \naround the region as the nose of the low level jet approaches and \nultimately folds over southern Wisconsin. Global ensemble \nguidance is in relatively sound agreement regarding low level \njet evolution & placement, providing further confidence for \nperiods of storms during this time frame. A broad overlap of \nsufficient shear and instability makes this a period to monitor \nfor organized/stronger thunderstorms, though whether these \nparameters come together to realize any potential will depend on\nsmaller-scale features & timing not yet captured by available \nguidance. Will be monitoring trends & providing additional info \nas this portion of the period draws closer. \n\nQuigley",
